window.GM_INFO = {
    confidence: ['Confidence', 'A grade from A to D combining how far the composite is from 50, whether the pillars agree with it, how many of the 70 settings put the sleeve in the same fifth, whether its data passed the checks, and how well the current setting has sorted sleeves in the backtest.', 'A and B are calls that hold up on several independent tests; C and D are leanings. The arrow gives the direction. It grades the strength of the evidence, not the size of the expected return.'],
    expRet: ['Expected 12-month return against the universe', 'The backtest\u2019s average relationship between this setting\u2019s score and the following 12 months\u2019 return over the universe average, applied to the sleeve\u2019s score, with a 90% range.', 'A calibrated translation of the score into return terms. The ranges are wide because the relationship is modest; read them as a sense of scale, not a forecast.'],
    fiTab: ['Fixed income', 'The Treasury curve and 27 bond ETFs across maturity, sector and credit quality: live curve and returns, weekly yields, durations and credit sensitivity.', 'The commentary follows the period selected; curve changes use the closest of one day, one week, one month, three months or one year. Returns against duration-matched Treasuries strip out the rate move to show the credit or sector effect alone.'],
    fiCarry: ['Compare any two measures', 'Every bond ETF on any two of its yield, risk, return, excess-return, trend or fund measures, with a least-squares line, correlation and R\u00b2. Click a category in the legend to hide or show it.', 'With duration across and yield up, the dashed line joins the Treasury ETFs, so the distance above it is the extra yield for credit, prepayment or currency risk. Excess returns subtract duration-matched Treasury returns.'],
    fiFund: ['Yield, duration and risk', 'Weekly, from FMP prices, distributions and fund data. See the Methodology page for how duration, equity beta and yield over Treasuries are estimated.', 'Distribution yields lag market yields, include TIPS inflation accruals, and are before tax for municipals.'],
    fi_pickup: ['Yield over Treasuries', 'Trailing 12-month yield less that of Treasury ETFs interpolated to the same empirical duration. A spread proxy, not an option-adjusted spread.'],
    fi_duration: ['Empirical duration', 'Sensitivity of weekly returns to the change in the best-fitting Treasury yield over three years, controlling for US equities. Near zero for floating-rate and loan funds.'],
    fi_equityBeta: ['Equity beta', 'Sensitivity of weekly returns to US equities over three years, controlling for Treasury yields. Higher means the fund trades more like credit risk than rate risk.'],
    maTab: ['Multi-asset performance', '34 assets across equities, government and core bonds, credit, real assets, commodities, currencies and bitcoin, plus cash and a 60/40 blend of global equities and US bonds, over any period from one day to twenty years.', 'Returns are dividend-adjusted total returns in US dollars to the live price, from ETFs (bitcoin from FMP\u2019s spot series, weekdays only). From two years on they are annualized. Up to two years come from live daily prices; three to twenty years and calendar years from the weekly rebuild. The 60/40 blend is 60% ACWI and 40% AGG, rebalanced monthly in the long-run figures and weighted by period return in the short-run ones. Correlations of stocks with Treasuries, gold and other assets use daily returns.'],
    maQuilt: ['Calendar-year returns, ranked', 'Each column ranks a representative of each asset class by its return that year, best at the top, colored by asset class. The current year runs to the latest weekly rebuild.', 'Read across a row to see how often an asset class leads or lags, and down a column to see how wide the spread between best and worst was.'],
    xsTab: ['Executive summary', 'Every model\u2019s current call side by side, what markets have done over the period you pick, what is changing, the main risks, and how much each model has earned in its backtest.', 'The stocks vs bonds, fixed income, real assets and currency models update with the Saturday rebuild; the equity rankings, markets and what is changing update with live prices.'],
    mpTab: ['Model portfolio', 'One set of fund weights from the allocation models: the stocks vs bonds weight, split into country funds and the best-scoring equity sleeves by the equity model, and into bond funds by the fixed income model, plus an optional diversifier sleeve chosen by the real assets model.', 'Equities: the policy neutral plus the band times the stocks vs bonds score, then ACWI country weights tilted by the equity scores (Equity allocation page settings); countries without a fund sit in ACWX. Then up to the sleeve budget (20% of equities by default, 5% at most per sleeve) moves into the best-scoring sector, industry, style, factor and regional sleeves, each funded from its own region so region weights do not change. Bonds: the Agg plus the fixed income pair trades. Diversifiers: the real assets with positive scores, in proportion. Reference: neutral weight in ACWI, the rest in AGG. Change the neutral, band and sleeve above; they apply to this page and the scenarios.'],
    mpRisk: ['Risk budget', 'How much each decision adds to the portfolio\u2019s tracking error against the reference.', 'Covariances from the last five years of monthly fund returns. Each decision is the difference it makes to the weights; its contribution is its covariance with the total active position divided by the tracking error, so contributions sum to the total.'],
    raTab: ['Real assets and commodities', 'Gold, broad commodities, US REITs and global infrastructure, scored as diversifiers for a 60/40 portfolio.', 'Live signals, each scaled to \u22121 to +1: trend for gold and commodities (12-month return and price against its 10-month average); an inflation nowcast for commodities; and diversification (correlation with 60/40 over three years) for all four. Gold\u2019s real yield and REITs\u2019 yield over the 10-year are shown as evidence only. The 12-month rank correlation shows how well the score has lined up with the asset\u2019s next-year return over 60/40 since 2009.'],
    fxTab: ['Currencies', 'Twelve currencies against the US dollar: returns, valuation and a suggested hedge ratio for developed-market equity holdings.', 'Daily closes from FMP, quoted as dollars per unit. Valuation against the currency\u2019s own 10-year average and against World Bank purchasing-power parity. Suggested hedge ratio: from the currency model, 50% moving toward 0% when it expects the currency to gain and toward 100% when it expects it to fall; where the model has no view, a rule based on valuation and the 12-month move.'],
    msTab: ['Model scorecard', 'All the models side by side, on the same footing.', 'Backtests are monthly, gross of trading costs, and use only data available at each date. The 90% range comes from resampling the monthly excess returns. The live record compounds each saved weekly call until the next one.'],
    combBt: ['The allocation models together', 'The stocks vs bonds model and the fixed income model run as one portfolio, against a static 60/40.', 'Each month: the model equity weight in ACWI, the rest in the Agg plus each fixed income tilt times its pair return. Monthly from December 2010, when at least five tilts have history; gross of costs; only data available at each date. The 90% range resamples 12-month blocks. The real assets variant moves 10% into the real assets the model favors.'],
    macroTab: ['Macro and regime', 'Where the economy is on growth, inflation and financial conditions, how data are landing against forecasts, what is coming, how crowded futures markets are, and how every asset has done in backdrops like this one.', 'Built every Saturday from FMP economic indicators, the economic calendar (actual against consensus), Treasury yields and market prices. Every input is scaled against its own history up to that month, so the regime history uses only what was known at the time. The same record feeds the allocation models, the results-by-backdrop tables, the scenarios and the weekly note.'],
    playbook: ['How the backdrop has played out', 'Average return a year in the month after each month-end that was in each state, for 35 assets, the 62 equity sleeves (against the average sleeve) or the allocation models (excess over their benchmarks).', 'States are judged with data available at each month-end, and returns are for the following month, so the table describes what followed a reading, not what coincided with it. Samples are small for some states; hover a cell for its months.'],
    surprise: ['Economic surprise indices', 'Whether data are beating or missing economists\u2019 forecasts, by region, for growth and for inflation.', 'Each surprise is the actual less the consensus forecast, scaled by that release\u2019s own past surprises, signed so good news is positive. Recent surprises count more (30-day half-life, 120-day window).'],
    positioning: ['Positioning in futures', 'How heavily speculators lean one way in 23 futures markets, against their own past three years.', 'Net non-commercial position as a share of open interest, from the weekly CFTC Commitments of Traders report. Beyond two standard deviations counts as crowded; the models read positioning as contrarian.'],
    labTab: ['Model lab', 'Re-runs the stocks vs bonds, fixed income and real assets models in the page from the stored monthly history of every signal, with your own pillar weights, signals and sizes.', 'The published settings re-run here reproduce the published backtests, which checks the lab. Results are split into the years up to 2018, which the promotion rule judges every signal on, and the years from 2020, which the rule only checks are not clearly negative. Neither is a clean out-of-sample test: the pillar weights and many design choices were made with the whole history in view. Trying many versions and keeping the best overstates skill: the selection haircut is the excess the best of that many versions with no skill would show by luck, tracking error \u00f7 \u221a(years) times the expected best of N draws from a normal distribution (about 0.6 for 2 versions, 1.5 for 10 and 2.2 for 50), and it is taken off the experiment\u2019s result. Versions of one model are related, so this overstates the luck a little: read it as a conservative charge.'],
    labSaved: ['Saved experiments', 'Experiments saved for everyone who can open the dashboard, with who saved them, when, and their result at the time.', 'The page cannot write to the dashboard\u2019s store itself, so experiments are saved by Claude: copy the settings and send them in chat or as a comment. The weekly refresh then records each saved experiment\u2019s call alongside the published models. An experiment made the published model is marked here with the date, and the previous settings stay listed as retired.'],
    callsTab: ['S&P 500 earnings calls', 'Every S&P 500 earnings call since 2014 that FMP has a transcript for, scored by Claude with a word list written for management speech: tone is positive less negative words as a share of both, skipping words negated just before; topics count phrases such as \u201craise prices\u201d or \u201ctariffs\u201d.', 'Readings use each company\u2019s latest call in the past three months, only for companies in the index on the call date (from FMP\u2019s record of index changes), so the history is not today\u2019s 500 carried back. Tone against own history compares a call with the same company\u2019s previous four, which removes differences in how companies talk. Transcript text is not kept, only the scores. Each reading was tested before use against what it should anticipate, on non-overlapping periods; those that did not pass are shown, not used.'],
    btWin: ['Which history', 'Full uses every month since January 2011. Before each fund\u2019s first holdings filing in FMP (the last arrived in late 2021), its earliest filing stands in, so those years use holdings the model could not have known.', 'Filed holdings only starts the month every sleeve has holdings as filed at the time, so nothing is carried back. It is about four years, so its ranges are wide: read it as a check on the full history, not a replacement. Every result on this page, the trust table on the summary and the Model scorecard follow the choice; the chart is rebased to 100 at the start of the window.'],
    fxmTab: ['Currency models', 'A cyclical view (6 to 12 months) and a long-term view (3 to 5 years) for 14 currencies against the dollar, the dollar index and an EM basket.', 'Cyclical: policy-rate gap and its 6-month change, growth-surprise gap, 12-1 month trend, futures positioning (contrarian), and haven sensitivity to financial conditions. Long-term: the real exchange rate against its 10-year average, and the real policy-rate gap. Each signal is scaled to \u22121 to +1 and averaged with equal weight. The dollar index is the weighted opposite of its main currencies (euro, yen, pound, Canadian dollar and Swiss franc); the EM basket averages seven.'],
    mpRegime: ['Model portfolio by backdrop', 'How today\u2019s model and reference weights would have done, on average, in months that were in each growth and inflation state since 2008.', 'Uses each fund\u2019s average return a year in the month after month-ends in that state. Country funds without their own history use the closest regional fund. A guide to sensitivity, not a forecast.'],
    scRegime: ['Replay a backdrop', 'Average returns in past months that were in the chosen growth and inflation state.', 'From the same regime history as the Macro page. Choose a state to see how each asset and the model portfolio fared.'],
    alCross: ['Country cross-checks', 'Valuation against required return, growth surprises and the currency view for each country fund.', 'Required return is the real 10-year Treasury yield plus FMP\u2019s total equity risk premium for the country (which includes its country risk premium). The gap compares that with the fund\u2019s cycle-adjusted earnings yield. FMP has no history for these premiums, so they cannot be tested and stay out of the score.'],
    registry: ['Signal registry', 'Every signal in the allocation models, its record and its status.', 'Rank correlation with the outcome each signal is meant to predict, 12 months ahead: before 2019 (signals dated to 2018) and from 2020. Live signals carry weight; evidence-only signals are computed and shown but do not move the call. The t-stats count one independent observation a year, since 12-month windows overlap; a t-stat below 2 means the record could easily be luck.'],
    sbTab: ['Stocks vs bonds', 'An equity weight for a global equities (ACWI) and US bonds (AGG) portfolio, around a 60/40 neutral and within 45% to 75%, from two live signals, each scored against its own history.', 'Live: the bond term premium (the 2s10s curve, reversed; valuation, 50%) and global growth surprises (macro, 25%). Momentum carries no weight, and no risk signal passed the promotion rule. Shown as evidence only: the US equity risk premium (S&P 500 members\u2019 cycle-adjusted earnings yield less the 10-year minus CPI), high yield against Treasuries over six months, the US growth nowcast, equity volatility and S&P futures positioning. Each input is a z-score against its own history, squashed to \u22121 to +1; the weight is 60% plus 15 points per unit of score. The backtest starts in June 2016.'],
    sbVar: ['Versions of the model', 'The same signals blended three ways. All three have added a little over a static 60/40 since mid-2016, within a range that includes zero.', 'The default was chosen on economic grounds before comparing results, but it has been seen alongside them, so treat any one version\u2019s record as an upper bound.'],
    sbIc: ['Which signals have worked', 'Rank correlation between each signal and the following return of global equities over US bonds, one month and twelve months ahead, monthly since mid-2016.', 'Positive means the signal pointed the right way. The curve (as a term premium) and global growth surprises have; momentum has not.'],
    frTab: ['Fixed income relative value', 'Seven tilts against the US Aggregate (duration, investment-grade credit, high yield, agency MBS, EM dollar debt, TIPS and EM local currency), each a pair of bond ETFs, sized to keep risk close to the Agg\u2019s.', 'Each lever buys what is cheap against its own history: a high real 10-year and a steep curve, each against its history, for duration; wide yield over Treasuries for high yield and EM dollar debt. Investment-grade credit and agency MBS have no live signal and stay at benchmark weight; rising inflation for TIPS; a dollar that has strengthened over six months for EM local currency. Positions: duration \u00b11.5 years through long against short Treasuries; IG \u00b115% and MBS \u00b110% against Treasuries of similar duration; high yield up to 10%, EM dollar up to 8%, TIPS up to 10% and EM local up to 5%, long only because the Agg holds none. Yield over Treasuries uses distribution yields, a proxy for spreads.'],
    emerging: ['What\u2019s changing', 'Recent market moves that run against the longer-term picture or mark a change in regime. Each line is triggered by a rule, so an empty list means nothing crossed a threshold.', 'Rules: a style, size or region pair whose one-month relative return reverses its 12-month direction by 2% or more; 12-month leaders down 3% or more against the median sleeve over a month, and laggards up as much; crosses of the 200-day average in the last ten sessions; new 52-week highs and lows in the last five; weekly moves beyond two standard deviations of the sleeve\u2019s own daily volatility; a 15-point change in the share of sleeves above their 200-day average; one-month return dispersion 50% above or 40% below its one-year average; rates, dollar, credit, oil and gold moving against their six-month direction or by more than two standard deviations in a week; top-fifth sleeves in the bottom fifth of one-month returns and the reverse; and monthly regime labels that changed over three months. Live: recalculated from the latest prices each time the page opens.'],
    catNotes: ['By category', 'For each category, which sleeves the data favors and why, which it does not, the valuation range and recent performance, written from the underlying numbers rather than the scores.', 'Evidence is drawn from the metrics that sit in the top or bottom quarter of the universe (or, for cycle-adjusted P/E, the top or bottom 30% of the sleeve\u2019s own history). Rankings follow the model settings above. A line headed Against the view flags a sleeve whose recent performance contradicts its ranking.'],
    shortTable: ['Every sleeve', 'Total returns over one day to twelve months, ending at the live price. Click any column heading to sort.', 'WTD runs from the last close before Monday. 1W is five trading days, 1M is 21. The 1W move in standard deviations divides the weekly return by the sleeve\u2019s daily volatility over the past three months times the square root of five; beyond two is unusual. Flags mark new 52-week highs or lows in the last five sessions and crosses of the 200-day average in the last ten.'],
    macroLive: ['Rates, currencies, credit and commodities', 'Live FMP data: Treasury yields and the 2s10s curve (changes in basis points), the dollar (UUP), high yield against Treasuries (HYG divided by IEF, dividend-adjusted), oil (USO), gold (GLD) and the VIX (change in points).', 'The 1-year percentile shows where today\u2019s level sits in its own past year. A weekly change beyond two standard deviations of the series\u2019 daily moves is set in bold.'],
    weeklyNote: ['Weekly note', 'A short committee-style summary written from the data at each Saturday rebuild: the top-down backdrop, the strongest calls, what changed, the live record and data flags.', 'It uses the default settings so that every week is comparable. A copy is also saved as a document after each weekly refresh.'],
    prefBoard: ['Preference board', 'For each theme pair, the composite gap under the current settings, the share of the 70 settings that agree on its direction, and how often a gap of at least 3 points called the direction of the next 12 months\u2019 relative return between 2011 and today.', 'A gap the settings agree on and the backtest supports is a theme call worth making; a gap that flips with the settings is a judgment about which stance you believe.'],
    scenario: ['Scenario lens', 'Estimated return of each sleeve in a scenario, from its sensitivities to global equities, the US 10-year yield, the dollar (UUP) and oil (USO), fitted jointly to the last five years of monthly returns.', 'Use it to see which preferences are really macro bets. The estimates assume the shocks arrive together and nothing else changes, and sensitivities shift over time.'],
    clusters: ['Separate bets', 'Sleeves are grouped by average-linkage clustering on one minus the correlation of 36 months of monthly returns. Groups whose members correlate at 0.85 or more on average count as one bet.', 'Three picks that are really one exposure carry one bet\u2019s worth of diversification. The count shows how concentrated a list of preferences really is.'],
    robustMap: ['Strength against robustness', 'Composite under the current settings against conviction: the share of 70 settings that put the sleeve in the top fifth less the share that put it in the bottom fifth.', 'The top-right corner holds strong calls that do not depend on your choice of weights; strong scores near the middle line depend on the stance.'],
    trailChart: ['Trail chart', 'Valuation and technicals scores at the last three month-ends from the rebuilt history, and today\u2019s live scores at the arrow.', 'Direction matters as much as position: a sleeve moving right and up is getting cheaper while its trend improves.'],
    waterfall: ['What moved', 'The change in each sleeve\u2019s composite between the last two weekly snapshots, split by pillar using the current weights.', 'Shows whether a move came from prices (technicals, sentiment) or from fundamentals and estimates, which change only at each rebuild.'],
    ledger: ['Call ledger', 'Entries into and exits from the top and bottom fifth at each weekly snapshot.', 'With the live track record, this is the model\u2019s out-of-sample history of calls. Frequent churn in the same names is a sign the call is marginal.'],
    capeH: ['Cycle-adjusted P/E in own range', 'Where today\u2019s cycle-adjusted P/E sits in the sleeve\u2019s own monthly record.', 'The default valuation read.'],
    shareholderYield: ['Shareholder yield', 'Dividend yield plus the reduction in diluted share count over the last year (or minus the increase), weighted across holdings.', 'Cash returned to shareholders through dividends and buybacks. Higher is cheaper. It counts in the within-category, cross-universe and blended valuation reads, not in the default cycle-adjusted read.'],
    netIssuance: ['Net share issuance', 'Weighted median change in diluted shares outstanding over the last year across holdings.', 'Dilution is a drag on per-share returns; persistent net issuance at the market level has historically preceded weaker returns. Lower is better; it counts in quality.'],
    marginTrend: ['Margin trend', 'Weighted median change in holdings\u2019 trailing net margin over the last year, excluding banks and insurers.', 'Improving profitability. Tested in the backtest and scored in growth only if it helped (see Methodology).'],
    fxValue: ['Currency valuation', 'Exposure-weighted cheapness of the currencies a fund holds against the dollar: each currency\u2019s price level from World Bank PPP conversion factors at today\u2019s exchange rate, compared with its peer group (developed or emerging) so poorer economies\u2019 naturally low price levels are not mistaken for cheapness. Dollar holdings count as zero.', 'A cheap currency is a tailwind to dollar returns over several years and a cushion against further weakness. Not backtested (no PPP history here), so it counts in the within-category, cross-universe and blended valuation reads only. Taiwan has no World Bank PPP, so Taiwan-heavy funds are left blank.'],
    fxMomentum: ['Currency trend', 'Exposure-weighted 12-month change of the fund\u2019s currencies against the dollar.', 'Shown for information; not scored.'],
    flow6m: ['Fund flows, 6 months', 'Growth in the ETF\u2019s shares outstanding over six months, a proxy for net creations and redemptions, from FMP market capitalization and price.', 'Crowding: heavy inflows mean investors have already piled in. Its direction and weight in sentiment follow the backtest (see Methodology). FMP smooths share counts between filings, so the backtest uses it three months late.'],
    expenseRatio: ['Expense ratio', 'The ETF\u2019s annual fee.', 'Shown for comparing sleeves; not scored.'],
    liveTrack: ['Live track record', 'Each weekly snapshot\u2019s top and bottom fifth, held until the next snapshot and chained, against all sleeves equally weighted, from dividend-adjusted closes.', 'This is the real test: nothing in it could have been fitted after the fact. Give it at least a year before reading much into it; a few months of returns are mostly noise.'],
    honesty: ['How much to trust the backtest', 'A 90% range from resampling whole 12-month blocks of history, turnover, and how the chosen default compares with every setting tested. Returns are gross of trading costs.', 'A backtest flatters whatever was chosen after seeing it. The median of all settings is a fairer guess at the future than the best one.'],
    erp: ['Equity risk premium', 'The cycle-adjusted earnings yield (100 divided by the cycle-adjusted P/E) less the US 10-year Treasury yield minus trailing CPI inflation. The same US real yield is used for every market.', 'Over multi-year horizons, a thin premium has tended to precede weaker equity returns relative to bonds. It is a slow, absolute gauge that sits outside the relative sleeve scores; it says nothing about the next few months. The real yield uses past inflation, not TIPS, so it is approximate.'],
    regime: ['Regimes', 'Each month since 2011 is labelled by the 10-year yield\u2019s six-month change (more than 25 basis points), the 10-year less 3-month curve, the dollar ETF UUP\u2019s six-month move (2%), high yield against Treasuries (HYG against IEF, 1%), US inflation against 3%, and ACWI against its 10-month average.', 'The table shows each pillar\u2019s 12-month rank correlation in months like each state. Samples are small, from a few dozen months, so use it to temper confidence rather than to switch weights.'],
    conviction: ['Conviction', 'How many of the 70 reasonable settings put a sleeve in the top or bottom fifth: seven weight presets, two sentiment readings and five ways of measuring valuation.', 'A sleeve in the top fifth under most settings is a robust call; one whose rank depends on the settings is a judgment about which stance you believe. The Bottom line lists sleeves in the top or bottom fifth under at least 60% of settings.'],
    capePE: ['Cycle-adjusted P/E', 'Today\u2019s price divided by the sleeve\u2019s average trailing earnings over the last five years (at least three), from its rebuilt monthly look-through history.', 'Trailing and forward P/E look cheapest exactly when earnings are at a cyclical peak, as in memory chips, energy or miners. Averaging earnings over a cycle removes most of that trap. Lower is cheaper.'],
    portfolio: ['Portfolio check', 'Scores your own equity holdings with the live equity model: weighted composite and pillars, the share of weight in each signal, holdings ranked low under most settings, uncorrelated sleeves you could add, and signal-tilted weights.', 'Signal-tilted weights scale each holding by one plus the maximum tilt times its score\u2019s distance from 50 (as a share of 50), with scores inside the neutral band treated as 50, then rescale to your total. Both settings are in the country tilts below.'],
    btSettings: ['Every setting, tested', 'For each preset and sentiment reading, the average 12-month return of the top and bottom score fifths over the universe average, measured at every month-end since 2011, plus the full-period return of a monthly-rebalanced top fifth.', 'Use it to see whether the stance you picked has held up historically, and how much it depends on the valuation basis. The backtest lacks the growth and analyst inputs, so it tests a simpler version of the live model.'],
    valBasis: ['How valuation is measured', 'Cycle-adjusted P/E vs own history (default): price over five-year average earnings, placed within the sleeve\u2019s own record since 2011, then ranked. Within category: each multiple ranked only against sleeves in the same group, averaged, then ranked across the universe. All multiples vs own history: where trailing P/E, EV/EBITDA, P/B, free cash flow yield and dividend yield sit within the sleeve\u2019s own monthly record since 2011. Across the universe: each multiple ranked against every other sleeve, the original method. Blend: the average of the three. Cycle-adjusted P/E, own history: only the five-year-average-earnings P/E, placed within the sleeve\u2019s own record.', 'Comparing a semiconductor fund\u2019s P/E with a Brazilian bank fund\u2019s mostly measures sector and country mix, which is why the cross-universe read pointed the wrong way in the backtest. The cycle-adjusted own-history read was the only method with a positive record, though its test starts only in late 2019. Forward P/E and PEG have no history, so they do not count in the own-history read.'],
    epsRevision13w: ['EPS estimate revision, 13 weeks', 'Weighted average change in holdings\u2019 consensus EPS for the current and next fiscal year over the last 13 weeks, from weekly snapshots of consensus saved since October 2026.', 'Rising estimates are one of the better-documented drivers of medium-term returns. It counts in the growth pillar once 13 weeks of snapshots exist; until then it is blank and drops out of the average.'],
    epsRevision4w: ['EPS estimate revision, 4 weeks', 'The same revision measure over the last four weeks.', 'Shown for information; not scored. Faster but noisier than the 13-week figure.'],
    revisionBreadth4w: ['Revision breadth, 4 weeks', 'Share of the fund, by weight, whose consensus EPS rose over four weeks, minus the share whose estimates fell.', 'Shown for information; not scored. Broad upgrades are more convincing than a few large ones.'],
    ratingDrift3m: ['Analyst rating drift, 3 months', 'Change over three months in the weighted share of buy ratings across holdings, from FMP\u2019s monthly rating history.', 'Shown for information; not scored. In the backtest it was slightly positive over one month but negative over 12 to 36 months, so it does not belong in a model with this horizon.'],
    dq: ['Data checks', 'Automatic checks run each time the snapshot is rebuilt, plus live checks on the quotes: broken or incomplete holdings files, thin data coverage, outlier multiples, likely currency mismatches in estimates, multiples that disagree with the sleeve\u2019s own history, and stale prices.', 'A flag does not mean a number is wrong; it marks where to look before acting on a score.'],
    sortBy: ['Sorting', 'Orders the sleeves in every chart, heatmap and table on the page: by the value shown, grouped by category, or alphabetically.', 'Grouping by category puts like with like, which is often the fairest comparison.'],
    recon: ['Reconstructed history', 'The model rebuilt at each month-end since 2011 from data that was available then: prices, quarterly financials with a 60-day reporting lag, and holdings breadth, using each fund\u2019s holdings as filed with the SEC at the time.', 'It has no consensus growth or analyst inputs, so fundamentals are quality only and sentiment is fund flows only. Use it to see how a sleeve\u2019s standing has moved, not as an exact replay of the live scores.'],
    breadthTab: ['Breadth', 'How broadly a fund\u2019s holdings are participating in its move, from live quotes on every holding in the look-through.', 'Narrow breadth under a rising price means a few large names are carrying the fund; broad breadth is a healthier trend. Shown for context; breadth is not scored.'],
    b50: ['Holdings above 50-day average', 'Share of the fund, by weight, trading above its own 50-day moving average.', 'A short-term participation gauge. Above about 70% is broad strength; below 30% is a broad pullback.'],
    b200: ['Holdings above 200-day average', 'Share of the fund, by weight, trading above its own 200-day moving average.', 'The standard long-term participation gauge, shown for context and not scored.'],
    bHigh: ['Near 52-week high', 'Share of the fund, by weight, within 5% of its own 52-week high.', 'Many names at highs is strength, and at extremes, crowding.'],
    bLow: ['Near 52-week low', 'Share of the fund, by weight, within 5% of its own 52-week low.', 'Many names at lows is weakness, and at extremes, capitulation.'],
    bNet: ['Highs minus lows', 'Weight near 52-week highs minus weight near 52-week lows.', 'Positive means more of the fund is pressing highs than lows.'],
    concTab: ['Concentration', 'How much a fund depends on a few holdings, sectors and countries, from its look-through holdings.', 'Concentrated funds carry more single-name risk and behave less like their label suggests. The S&P 500 is now far more concentrated than its 500 names imply.'],
    top10: ['Top-10 weight', 'Combined weight of the ten largest holdings.', 'Above about 50% the fund is effectively a basket of its leaders.'],
    effN: ['Effective holdings', 'One divided by the sum of squared weights: the number of equal-weight positions that would give the same concentration. Holdings beyond the look-through are assumed equally weighted.', 'A 500-stock fund with 45 effective holdings diversifies like a 45-stock equal-weight portfolio.'],
    vol10: ['Volatility', 'Annualized standard deviation of weekly returns over the last ten years, or since the fund started.', 'Context for the returns beside it.'],
    seriesTab: ['Time series', 'Monthly history of each sleeve\u2019s look-through multiples, quality metrics, breadth and rebuilt scores since 2011, prices since 2005, category medians, and quarterly multiples for the largest holdings.', 'Multiples use today\u2019s holdings for every month, so the history shows how the current portfolio has been valued over time, not how the fund was valued then.'],
    btTab: ['Backtest', 'At each month-end since 2011 the model is rebuilt from each fund\u2019s holdings as filed at the time, sleeves are ranked, and portfolios are held for the next month. Strategies are equal-weighted unless stated and ignore costs.', 'A test of whether the ranking has historically pointed to better sleeves, with the caveats listed at the bottom of the tab.'],
    ic: ['Rank correlation', 'For each month, the correlation between sleeves\u2019 scores and their subsequent returns, averaged across months. Also called the information coefficient.', 'Around 0.05 is useful for a cross-asset signal; above 0.10 is strong. Negative means the signal pointed the wrong way.'],
    presets: ['Pillar weights', 'How the four pillars are blended into the composite. Presets: equal; value tilt 30/40/15/15; momentum 15/15/50/20; quality 45/25/15/15; contrarian 20/35/10/35; long horizon 30/35/20/15; backtest-informed 50/10/20/20 (fundamentals, valuation, technicals, sentiment). Fitted to 6, 12 or 36 months: weights from each pillar\u2019s rank correlation at that horizon, shrunk halfway to equal. Custom takes any four weights and rescales them to 100%.', 'Changing the weights changes the composite, ranks, signals, deployment picks, allocation tilts and history on the equity pages.'],
    signal: ['Signals', 'Overweight when the composite is at or above the first threshold, Underweight at or below the second, Neutral in between.', 'A signal is a prompt to look closer, not an instruction. Tighter thresholds give fewer, higher-conviction signals.'],
    deploy: ['Deploy cash', 'Ranks every asset the models cover (62 equity sleeves, the bond funds a fixed income lever trades, and four real assets) and takes them in order, skipping any whose daily-return correlation over the past year with an earlier pick is above the maximum.', 'Scores are on a 0 to 100 scale with 50 neutral: the equity composite as it stands; for bonds and real assets, 50 plus 50 times the model signal. Top overall takes the highest scores wherever they are; the per-slice views take the best in each asset class, equity region, equity category or bond sector. Split by signal strength sizes each pick by how far its score sits above 50; Follow the model portfolio mix first splits the money by the model portfolio\u2019s equity, bond and diversifier shares, then by score within each.'],
    maxCorr: ['Maximum correlation', 'The highest 36-month correlation of monthly returns allowed between any two picks.', 'Lower values force more diversified picks. Around 0.85 rules out near-duplicates such as two US small-cap funds; around 0.6 forces picks from different regions or styles.'],
    relations: ['Strongest relationships', 'Every pair of metrics ranked by R², the share of one metric\u2019s variation across sleeves that the other explains.', 'Pairs from different pillars with a high R² mean two signals are telling you the same thing, so the composite may be counting it twice. Pairs within a pillar are expected to move together.'],
    relStrength: ['Relative strength', 'A sleeve\u2019s month-end price divided by the benchmark\u2019s, rebased to 100 at the start of the window, using dividend-adjusted prices.', 'A rising line is outperformance. Leadership changes over 12 to 36 months often show up here before they show up in the scores.'],
    corr: ['Correlation', 'Correlation of monthly returns over the latest 36 months, from \u22121 to 1.', 'Below about 0.5 is a meaningful diversifier; above 0.9 two sleeves are close to the same exposure.'],
    tilt: ['Country tilts', 'Each country\u2019s ACWI weight is multiplied by one plus the maximum tilt times its score\u2019s distance from 50 as a share of 50, then all weights are rescaled to 100%. Scores inside the neutral band count as 50.', 'A maximum tilt of 50% means a top-scoring country can reach up to one and a half times its benchmark weight before rescaling, and a bottom-scoring one half of it.'],
    history: ['Score history', 'Saved snapshots of every sleeve\u2019s pillar scores and composites, compared with today\u2019s live scores under the same weights and sentiment reading.', 'A sleeve moving by more than the band is labelled improving or fading. Price moves change scores daily; fundamentals change when the snapshot is rebuilt.'],
    signalCol: ['Signal', 'Overweight, Neutral or Underweight, from the composite and the thresholds in the equity settings at the top of the page.', 'Thresholds are adjustable.'],
    ret1m: ['1-month return', 'Dividend-adjusted total return over the last 21 trading days.', 'Context only; not part of any score.'],
    ret3m: ['3-month return', 'Dividend-adjusted total return over the last 63 trading days.', 'Context only; not part of any score.'],
    retYtd: ['Year-to-date return', 'Dividend-adjusted total return since the last close of the prior year.', 'Context only; not part of any score.'],
    ret12m: ['1-year return', 'Dividend-adjusted total return over the last 252 trading days.', 'Context only; not part of any score.'],
    composite: ['Composite score', 'Weighted average of the four pillars, using the pillar weights in the equity settings (equal by default).', 'A sleeve ranks high when several independent lenses agree. A high composite carried by one pillar is less robust than a balanced one, so read it alongside the pillar columns.'],
    fundamentals: ['Fundamentals', 'Average of the quality and growth sub-scores, built bottom-up from the fund’s holdings.', 'Over 12 to 36 months, earnings growth and the durability of those earnings drive most of an equity market’s return after valuation. Higher is better.'],
    quality: ['Quality', 'Profitability, how well earnings are backed by cash, balance-sheet leverage and how predictable earnings are.', 'High-quality earnings compound and hold up better in downturns. Higher is better.'],
    growth: ['Growth', 'Consensus expectations for earnings and revenue growth over the current and next fiscal year, and how broad that growth is.', 'Growth is what lets a market grow into its multiple. Higher is better, but read it next to valuation: fast growth is often already priced.'],
    valuation: ['Valuation', 'What you pay per unit of earnings, cash flow, book value and dividends. By default it is the cycle-adjusted P/E (price over five-year average earnings) against the sleeve\u2019s own history; the Valuation control switches to within-category, all-multiples-vs-own-history, cross-universe or blended reads.', 'Starting valuation is one of the more reliable guides to multi-year returns, though a weak timing tool over months. Higher score means cheaper.'],
    technicals: ['Technicals', 'Price trend and momentum: medium-term returns, position against moving averages, drawdown and volatility.', 'Momentum tends to persist over roughly 3 to 12 months, and a trend filter helps avoid buying into a falling market. Higher means a stronger, steadier trend.'],
    sentiment: ['Sentiment', 'Positioning: how many analysts rate the holdings a buy, how far price targets sit above prices, and how much money has flowed into the fund over six months.', 'Read contrarian (the default), crowded optimism and heavy inflows score low and neglect scores high, because over 12 to 36 months crowded positions tend to disappoint. In the backtest, contrarian fund flows had a small positive record. Read confirming, the scale flips. Price breadth and RSI are no longer part of it: read contrarian they pointed the wrong way.'],
    sentMode: ['How sentiment is read', 'Contrarian scores a sleeve higher when positioning is washed out and lower when it is crowded. Confirming flips every sentiment metric, so optimism scores higher.', 'Contrarian is the default for a 12 to 36 month horizon: once nearly everyone is bullish, good news is priced in and marginal buyers are scarce. Confirming suits shorter horizons, but then it overlaps heavily with technicals. Each mode has its own composite and group rank.'],
    groupRank: ['Rank in group', 'Where the sleeve’s composite ranks among sleeves in its own group, such as sectors or emerging countries.', 'Use it to compare like with like. 1 is the best in its group.'],
    dayChange: ['Today', 'Live price change today, from FMP.', 'Shown for context only and not part of any score.'],
    roe: ['Return on equity', 'Net income divided by shareholders’ equity, trailing twelve months. Fund value is the weighted median of its holdings.', 'Higher is better: more profit per dollar of owners’ capital. Very high ROE can come from leverage or buybacks shrinking equity, so check net debt alongside it.'],
    roic: ['Return on invested capital', 'After-tax operating profit divided by debt plus equity invested in the business. Banks and insurers are excluded.', 'Higher is better. Returns well above the cost of capital are the clearest sign of a business that creates value as it grows.'],
    netMargin: ['Net margin', 'Net income as a share of revenue, weighted median of holdings.', 'Higher is better: it signals pricing power and cost control, and a cushion when demand slows. Sector mix drives much of it, so compare similar sleeves.'],
    cashConversion: ['Cash conversion', 'Operating cash flow divided by net income. Banks and insurers are excluded.', 'Higher is better. Around 1 or above means reported earnings turn into cash. Persistently below 1 is a classic earnings-quality warning, pointing to aggressive accruals.'],
    netDebtEbitda: ['Net debt to EBITDA', 'Debt minus cash, divided by EBITDA. Banks and insurers are excluded; negative means net cash.', 'Lower is better: less refinancing risk when rates are high and more room to keep investing through a downturn.'],
    estimateDispersion: ['Earnings visibility', 'The spread between the highest and lowest analyst estimate for this year’s EPS, divided by the average. Weighted median of holdings.', 'Lower is better. When analysts agree, earnings are more predictable and less likely to disappoint. Wide spreads mean the outlook is genuinely uncertain.'],
    epsGrowthFy1: ['EPS growth, this fiscal year', 'Consensus EPS growth from the last completed fiscal year to the current one, earnings-weighted across holdings.', 'Higher is better. Much of this year is already reported, so it says more about momentum in earnings than about the future.'],
    epsGrowthFy2: ['EPS growth, next fiscal year', 'Consensus EPS growth from the current fiscal year to the next one.', 'Higher is better. This is the most forward-looking growth figure and the one least known to the market.'],
    epsCagr2y: ['EPS growth, 2-year annualized', 'Annualized consensus EPS growth from the last completed fiscal year to the next one.', 'Higher is better. It is the core growth gauge for a 12 to 36 month view and feeds the PEG ratio.'],
    revCagr2y: ['Revenue growth, 2-year annualized', 'Annualized consensus revenue growth over the same two years, sales-weighted.', 'Higher is better. Revenue is harder to flatter than EPS with buybacks or margin cuts, so it shows whether growth is real demand.'],
    estimateBreadth: ['Growth breadth', 'Share of the fund, by weight, where analysts expect next year’s EPS to beat this year’s.', 'Higher is better. Growth spread across many companies is more durable than growth concentrated in a few large names.'],
    fwdPE: ['Forward P/E', 'Price divided by consensus EPS for the next twelve months, blending this year and next by time remaining. Aggregated as an index would be (harmonic).', 'Lower is cheaper and scores higher. Read it with growth: a higher multiple can be justified by faster growth, which the PEG ratio adjusts for.'],
    peTTM: ['Trailing P/E', 'Price divided by the last twelve months of reported EPS.', 'Lower is cheaper. It is backward-looking and distorted by one-offs and by cyclical peaks and troughs in earnings.'],
    evEbitda: ['EV to EBITDA', 'Enterprise value (market value plus net debt) divided by EBITDA. Banks and insurers are excluded.', 'Lower is cheaper. Because it includes debt, it compares companies with different balance sheets on equal terms.'],
    pb: ['Price to book', 'Market value divided by the accounting value of equity.', 'Lower is cheaper. Most useful for banks and asset-heavy industries; it understates the value of software and brands.'],
    fcfYield: ['Free cash flow yield', 'Free cash flow divided by market value. Banks and insurers are excluded.', 'Higher is better: more cash available for dividends, buybacks and paying down debt for each dollar invested.'],
    divYield: ['Dividend yield', 'Trailing dividends divided by price.', 'Higher is better as a sign of cheapness and cash return. Very high yields can signal a market pricing in distress.'],
    peg: ['PEG ratio', 'Forward P/E divided by 2-year annualized EPS growth in percent. Only shown when growth is above 2%.', 'Lower is better: it asks how much you pay per point of growth, so fast growers are not penalized just for a higher P/E.'],
    mom12_1: ['12-month momentum, skip last month', 'Total return from twelve months ago to one month ago, dividend-adjusted.', 'Higher is better. This is the standard academic momentum signal. Skipping the latest month avoids the short-term reversal that tends to follow sharp moves.'],
    ret6m: ['6-month return', 'Total return over the last six months, dividend-adjusted.', 'Higher is better: a medium-term trend that tends to persist.'],
    vs200d: ['Price vs 200-day average', 'How far the price is above or below its 200-day moving average.', 'Higher is better here, as a trend filter: above the line means the long-term trend is up. Extreme readings across holdings are handled in sentiment.'],
    sma50vs200: ['50-day vs 200-day average', 'The 50-day moving average relative to the 200-day.', 'Higher is better: the medium-term trend is running above the long-term one (the so-called golden cross).'],
    drawdown52w: ['Distance from 52-week high', 'How far the price sits below its highest close of the last year.', 'Closer to zero is better: shallow drawdowns mean an intact trend. Deep ones mean the trend is broken until proven otherwise.'],
    vol3m: ['Volatility, 3-month', 'Annualized standard deviation of daily returns over the last three months.', 'Lower is better: steadier trends tend to persist, and lower volatility means a better risk-adjusted ride.'],
    buyShare: ['Analyst buy ratings', 'Share of analyst ratings on the holdings that are buy or strong buy, weighted. Coverage is mostly US-listed companies.', 'Contrarian: lower scores higher, because when nearly every analyst is already positive, good news is priced in and upgrades are used up. Confirming flips this.'],
    targetUpside: ['Upside to price target', 'Consensus 12-month analyst price target versus today’s live price, weighted across holdings.', 'Contrarian: lower scores higher, since large upside means the sell side is more optimistic than the market. Large upside also appears right after a sell-off, before targets are cut. Confirming reads high upside as room to run.'],
    near52wHigh: ['Holdings near 52-week high', 'Share of the fund, by weight, whose holdings trade within 5% of their own 52-week high. Live.', 'A participation gauge, shown on the Breadth tab and not scored: it moves with momentum, and read contrarian it pointed the wrong way in the backtest.'],
    above200d: ['Holdings above 200-day average', 'Share of the fund, by weight, whose holdings trade above their own 200-day average. Live.', 'A participation gauge, shown on the Breadth tab and not scored. Broad participation has tended to accompany healthy trends; read contrarian it pointed the wrong way in the backtest.'],
    rsi14: ['RSI, 14-day', 'Relative strength index of the ETF over 14 trading days, from 0 to 100.', 'A short-term overbought or oversold gauge, shown for information and not scored.'],
  };
